1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:01,823
Your server is slow.

2
00:00:02,373 --> 00:00:04,719
Is it the processor, the memory, or the disk?

3
00:00:05,269 --> 00:00:06,613
Guessing wastes hours.

4
00:00:07,163 --> 00:00:10,641
Today you learn what each part does, and how programs share them.

5
00:00:11,191 --> 00:00:16,076
That is processors, memory, storage, processes, threads, and resource contention.

6
00:00:18,126 --> 00:00:19,577
First, the CPU.

7
00:00:20,127 --> 00:00:23,796
The CPU runs instructions, one tiny step at a time.

8
00:00:24,346 --> 00:00:28,591
Modern ones have several cores, and each core works on one thing at a time.

9
00:00:29,141 --> 00:00:31,616
More cores means more work at the same moment.

10
00:00:32,166 --> 00:00:35,153
A busy CPU means work is waiting for its turn.

11
00:00:37,203 --> 00:00:39,272
Next, memory, also called RAM.

12
00:00:39,822 --> 00:00:42,532
Memory holds what programs are using right now.

13
00:00:43,082 --> 00:00:45,300
It is very fast, but it is temporary.

14
00:00:45,850 --> 00:00:48,432
Switch off the machine, and memory is emptied.

15
00:00:48,982 --> 00:00:54,080
When memory runs out, the system slows down badly, or stops programs to survive.

16
00:00:56,130 --> 00:00:57,559
Then storage, the disk.

17
00:00:58,109 --> 00:01:02,589
Storage keeps data permanently: files, programs, and databases.

18
00:01:03,139 --> 00:01:05,699
It is much larger than memory, and much slower.

19
00:01:06,249 --> 00:01:08,895
Switch off the machine, and the data is still there.

20
00:01:09,445 --> 00:01:13,797
When storage fills up, programs cannot save, and logs cannot be written.

21
00:01:14,347 --> 00:01:19,360
Remember the trade: memory is fast and temporary, storage is slower and lasting.

22
00:01:21,410 --> 00:01:22,562
Now, processes.

23
00:01:23,112 --> 00:01:27,720
A program is a file, waiting on disk. A process is that program, running.

24
00:01:28,270 --> 00:01:31,555
Open the same program twice, and you get two processes.

25
00:01:32,105 --> 00:01:35,604
Each process has its own identity number, called a PID.

26
00:01:36,154 --> 00:01:39,098
Each has its own memory, kept separate from the others.

27
00:01:39,648 --> 00:01:44,427
The operating system decides which process gets the CPU, and for how long.

28
00:01:46,477 --> 00:01:48,632
Inside a process, there can be threads.

29
00:01:49,182 --> 00:01:52,019
A thread is one line of work, inside a process.

30
00:01:52,569 --> 00:01:55,001
All the threads of a process share its memory.

31
00:01:55,551 --> 00:01:59,796
That lets one program do many things at once, like serving many customers.

32
00:02:00,346 --> 00:02:04,272
But shared memory means the program must manage its threads carefully.

33
00:02:06,322 --> 00:02:08,797
Now the key idea: resource contention.

34
00:02:09,347 --> 00:02:13,997
Contention is when several processes want the same resource, at the same time.

35
00:02:14,547 --> 00:02:18,537
Two cores, and four busy programs: each gets about half of a core.

36
00:02:19,087 --> 00:02:21,753
Nothing is broken. Everything is just slower.

37
00:02:22,303 --> 00:02:26,335
The system switches between them so quickly that they seem to run together.

38
00:02:26,885 --> 00:02:29,595
Memory has contention too. So does the disk.

39
00:02:31,645 --> 00:02:35,272
Now let us see this for real, in a disposable Linux sandbox.

40
00:02:35,822 --> 00:02:39,022
These outputs are real captures, not an illustration.

41
00:02:39,572 --> 00:02:44,009
First: this machine has two processors, and about eight gigabytes of memory.

42
00:02:44,559 --> 00:02:47,951
Next, we start four programs that do nothing but calculate.

43
00:02:48,501 --> 00:02:54,453
Look at the CPU column. Four processes share two cores, so each gets about fifty percent.

44
00:02:55,003 --> 00:02:56,688
That is contention, measured.

45
00:02:57,238 --> 00:03:02,465
Last: one Python process, with five threads. That is the main thread, plus four more.

46
00:03:03,015 --> 00:03:06,023
One process, many threads, all sharing its memory.

47
00:03:08,073 --> 00:03:09,929
Why does this matter for DevOps?

48
00:03:10,479 --> 00:03:12,612
Because slow has three common causes.

49
00:03:13,162 --> 00:03:16,896
CPU contention: processes wait for processor time.

50
00:03:17,446 --> 00:03:21,136
Memory shortage: the system slows down, or stops programs.

51
00:03:21,686 --> 00:03:24,310
Full storage: writes fail, and logs stop.

52
00:03:24,860 --> 00:03:26,439
Measure first, then fix.

53
00:03:28,489 --> 00:03:30,345
Watch out for four common myths.

54
00:03:30,895 --> 00:03:33,788
Myth one: more memory makes everything faster.

55
00:03:33,788 --> 00:03:34,292
In fact,

56
00:03:34,292 --> 00:03:36,996
it helps only when memory was the shortage.

57
00:03:37,546 --> 00:03:40,217
Myth two: memory and storage are the same.

58
00:03:40,217 --> 00:03:40,726
In fact,

59
00:03:40,726 --> 00:03:42,379
one is fast and temporary,

60
00:03:42,379 --> 00:03:44,224
the other slower and lasting.

61
00:03:44,774 --> 00:03:47,855
Myth three: a program and a process are the same.

62
00:03:47,855 --> 00:03:48,358
In fact,

63
00:03:48,358 --> 00:03:50,811
a process is a program that is running.

64
00:03:51,361 --> 00:03:54,477
Myth four: a busy CPU is always a problem.

65
00:03:54,477 --> 00:03:55,071
In fact,

66
00:03:55,071 --> 00:03:58,038
work waiting for the CPU is the problem.

67
00:03:58,588 --> 00:03:59,932
Measure, then decide.

68
00:04:01,982 --> 00:04:04,307
Here is how you check your own understanding.

69
00:04:04,857 --> 00:04:07,460
Imagine a friend says: my server is slow.

70
00:04:08,010 --> 00:04:11,573
Explain what the CPU, memory, and storage each do.

71
00:04:12,123 --> 00:04:13,957
Explain a process, and a thread.

72
00:04:14,507 --> 00:04:17,515
Explain contention, with an example and numbers.

73
00:04:18,065 --> 00:04:20,561
Say which resource you would measure first, and why.

74
00:04:21,111 --> 00:04:22,797
Check yourself on six points.

75
00:04:23,347 --> 00:04:24,968
To pass, you need all six.

76
00:04:27,018 --> 00:04:27,893
Your turn.

77
00:04:28,443 --> 00:04:31,365
Take any computer you can reach, and find three things.

78
00:04:31,915 --> 00:04:35,115
One: how many processors it has, and how much memory.

79
00:04:35,665 --> 00:04:38,929
Two: which process is using the most CPU right now.

80
00:04:39,479 --> 00:04:44,215
On Windows, Task Manager shows both. On Linux, the commands from the demo do.

81
00:04:44,765 --> 00:04:46,771
Three: mark yourself on the rubric.

82
00:04:47,321 --> 00:04:49,049
Pause the video, and do it now.

83
00:05:02,599 --> 00:05:03,474
Quick check.

84
00:05:04,024 --> 00:05:06,541
What is the difference between memory and storage?

85
00:05:07,091 --> 00:05:09,694
What is the difference between a process and a thread?

86
00:05:10,244 --> 00:05:11,822
What is resource contention?

87
00:05:12,372 --> 00:05:14,804
Name three common causes of a slow server.

88
00:05:15,354 --> 00:05:19,792
Objective covered: MC02-O02.

89
00:05:20,342 --> 00:05:23,862
Pause the video, and explain contention with your own example.

90
